1
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:04,881
Every October, the same story returns.

2
00:00:05,181 --> 00:00:09,050
Halloween, we are told,
began as Samhain, an ancient

3
00:00:09,050 --> 00:00:13,071
Celtic festival of the dead,
ruled by a lord of death.

4
00:00:13,371 --> 00:00:15,005
It is a good story.

5
00:00:15,305 --> 00:00:17,760
But what do the sources actually say?

6
00:00:19,667 --> 00:00:25,110
In this episode, we follow the paper
trail: medieval Irish manuscripts,

7
00:00:25,110 --> 00:00:31,331
a Christian feast day, a Scottish poem,
and a short newspaper report from Canada.

8
00:00:31,631 --> 00:00:37,264
Halloween turns out to be not one ancient
festival, but several layers of history.

9
00:00:42,667 --> 00:00:47,038
The word Samhain appears in early
Irish literature, written down

10
00:00:47,038 --> 00:00:51,825
by Christian scribes between roughly
the eighth and twelfth centuries.

11
00:00:52,125 --> 00:00:55,670
In these texts, Samhain marks
the beginning of winter,

12
00:00:55,670 --> 00:00:58,546
one of the great turning
points of the year.

13
00:00:59,467 --> 00:01:03,131
The tales describe assemblies
and feasts at Samhain,

14
00:01:03,131 --> 00:01:07,155
including gatherings at Tara,
the seat of the high kings.

15
00:01:07,455 --> 00:01:11,721
Some stories say that at Samhain,
the mounds of the otherworld

16
00:01:11,721 --> 00:01:16,197
stood open, and beings from the other
side could cross into ours.

17
00:01:17,100 --> 00:01:21,599
That is where the idea of a night
when worlds draw close comes from.

18
00:01:21,899 --> 00:01:25,069
But these are stories,
written down centuries

19
00:01:25,069 --> 00:01:28,094
after the pagan period,
by Christian hands.

20
00:01:28,394 --> 00:01:33,703
They are not eyewitness accounts of pagan
rites, and historians such as Ronald

21
00:01:33,703 --> 00:01:39,356
Hutton have noted how little they reveal
about any religious ceremonies at Samhain.

22
00:01:41,733 --> 00:01:46,237
Older still is a bronze calendar
found at Coligny in France,

23
00:01:46,237 --> 00:01:49,825
made in Roman Gaul about
two thousand years ago.

24
00:01:50,125 --> 00:01:54,222
The museum that holds it dates
it to the late first century.

25
00:01:54,522 --> 00:01:57,411
One of its months is called Samonios.

26
00:01:57,711 --> 00:02:01,251
The name may be related
to Samhain, but the calendar

27
00:02:01,251 --> 00:02:05,068
tells us almost nothing about
how that month was marked.

28
00:02:07,700 --> 00:02:09,417
And the lord of the dead?

29
00:02:09,717 --> 00:02:13,373
In the late eighteenth century,
the antiquarian Charles

30
00:02:13,373 --> 00:02:16,893
Vallancey claimed that Samhain
was the name of a god.

31
00:02:17,193 --> 00:02:19,478
He gave no ancient source for it,

32
00:02:19,478 --> 00:02:22,940
and his theories were doubted
even in his own time.

33
00:02:23,867 --> 00:02:28,834
Still, the idea spread, and books
in the twentieth century repeated it.

34
00:02:29,134 --> 00:02:32,409
But there is no such god
in early Irish texts.

35
00:02:32,709 --> 00:02:37,147
Samhain was the name of a season
and a festival, not of a deity.

36
00:02:40,167 --> 00:02:43,410
The name Halloween comes
from another tradition.

37
00:02:43,710 --> 00:02:48,263
In the Christian calendar, the first
of November is All Saints' Day.

38
00:02:48,563 --> 00:02:54,013
According to Encyclopaedia Britannica,
Pope Gregory the Third dedicated

39
00:02:54,013 --> 00:02:59,307
a chapel in Rome to all the saints
in the eighth century, and in 837,

40
00:02:59,307 --> 00:03:04,446
Pope Gregory the Fourth ordered
the feast to be observed generally.

41
00:03:05,367 --> 00:03:10,550
In medieval England, the feast was called
All Hallows, hallow meaning saint.

42
00:03:10,850 --> 00:03:15,978
The evening before was All Hallows' Eve,
shortened over time to Hallowe'en.

43
00:03:16,278 --> 00:03:18,574
So the name itself is Christian.

44
00:03:23,233 --> 00:03:25,929
Historians still debate whether the date

45
00:03:25,929 --> 00:03:28,895
of the feast had anything
to do with Samhain.

46
00:03:29,195 --> 00:03:33,179
What we can say is that in Ireland
and Scotland, the start

47
00:03:33,179 --> 00:03:37,652
of winter and the eve of All Saints
came to share the same night.

48
00:03:40,467 --> 00:03:44,369
One of the richest early
descriptions comes from Scotland.

49
00:03:44,669 --> 00:03:48,920
In 1785, Robert Burns
wrote a long poem called

50
00:03:48,920 --> 00:03:52,699
Halloween, published the following year.

51
00:03:52,999 --> 00:03:56,941
He described young people pulling
up kale stalks in the dark,

52
00:03:56,941 --> 00:04:00,751
and burning nuts by the fire
to learn who they would marry.

53
00:04:03,433 --> 00:04:08,135
Burns even added notes to explain
the customs to his readers.

54
00:04:08,435 --> 00:04:12,146
In his poem, Halloween
is less about fear than about

55
00:04:12,146 --> 00:04:15,494
laughter, mischief,
and guessing at the future.

56
00:04:15,794 --> 00:04:20,390
It is a picture of a rural
community, not of an ancient rite.

57
00:04:22,200 --> 00:04:24,506
Then there is the jack-o'-lantern.

58
00:04:24,806 --> 00:04:29,204
In seventeenth-century England,
the phrase meant a man carrying

59
00:04:29,204 --> 00:04:33,390
a lantern, or the strange lights
seen over marshes at night.

60
00:04:33,690 --> 00:04:36,305
Only in the nineteenth century does it

61
00:04:36,305 --> 00:04:39,333
appear for a lantern
carved from a vegetable.

62
00:04:40,233 --> 00:04:43,660
In parts of Ireland
and Scotland, those lanterns

63
00:04:43,660 --> 00:04:46,650
were carved from turnips and other roots.

64
00:04:46,950 --> 00:04:50,717
When the custom crossed
the Atlantic, people in North

65
00:04:50,717 --> 00:04:55,063
America used pumpkins, which
were larger and easier to carve.

66
00:04:55,967 --> 00:04:58,613
Trick-or-treat has a similar story.

67
00:04:58,913 --> 00:05:02,983
Going from house to house
in disguise, known as guising,

68
00:05:02,983 --> 00:05:07,349
was part of Halloween in Scotland
by the nineteenth century.

69
00:05:07,649 --> 00:05:12,240
But the earliest known use
of the phrase trick-or-treat

70
00:05:12,240 --> 00:05:17,086
comes from a newspaper report
in Alberta, Canada, in 1927.

71
00:05:17,386 --> 00:05:20,577
In the decades that followed,
Halloween parties

72
00:05:20,577 --> 00:05:24,114
and door-to-door visits
spread across North America.

73
00:05:26,433 --> 00:05:28,433
So where does Halloween come from?

74
00:05:28,733 --> 00:05:31,231
Not from a single ancient festival.

75
00:05:31,531 --> 00:05:34,639
It grew from a Gaelic
turning of the seasons,

76
00:05:34,639 --> 00:05:38,594
a Christian feast, rural
fortune-telling, and the customs

77
00:05:38,594 --> 00:05:41,984
of emigrants who carried
them to a new continent.

78
00:05:44,600 --> 00:05:47,691
Each layer left its trace
in the sources, and each

79
00:05:47,691 --> 00:05:51,097
tells us something about
the people who kept the night.

80
00:05:51,397 --> 00:05:55,692
Every story has an origin,
and sometimes, more than one.

81
00:05:58,300 --> 00:06:03,118
This video is based on historical
sources and public collections.

82
00:06:03,418 --> 00:06:06,311
The narration uses synthetic speech,

83
00:06:06,311 --> 00:06:10,167
and some images are
AI-generated reconstructions.

84
00:06:10,467 --> 00:06:14,413
Sources and terms of use are
listed in the description.

85
00:06:14,713 --> 00:06:16,465
Thank you for watching.
